  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Heat builds again mid to late week but not as \nhot as last week. A chance of storms Thu-Sat.\n\nToday, a surface high pressure system will advect drier air \nlooks to settle further into the Mid- Atlantic states and the \nSoutheast. This will effectively cut off any precipitation \nchances through the middle of this week. Temperatures also look\nto moderate to near normal to slightly above normal values \nthrough Tuesday as an upper level trough keeps the upper level \nridge that led to last weeks heat wave over the central Conus \nto our west through this period. By Wednesday; however, the \nridge looks to slide back east, and temperatures look to \nincrease to above normal values once again as 500mb heights\nincrease to around 594dm. This should lead to temperatures about\n5-10 degrees above normal for this time of the year. \n\nAnother cold front looks to approach the region by the late\nThursday/ early Friday timeframe; however, the exact timing of \nthis front is still uncertain. A passage of the front during the\nearly afternoon and evening would lead to a higher chance for\nsevere weather across the area; however, a passage late in the\nevening and during the overnight hours into Friday morning would\nlead to less of a severe weather threat. This front looks to\nstall across the Carolinas on Friday and Saturday as it becomes\nparallel with the upper level flow, which will keep shower\nchances in the forecast through the weekend. This front will be\nthe next best chance for rain across the region. Temperatures \nlook to cool off once again to near to just above normal for \nthis time of the year by next weekend.",
